 teach: {
  prompt: "Explain to a new assistant why nitrous oxide works fast, why the patient gets pure oxygen at the end, and why it must not be used in someone with a pneumothorax. Use one physical idea for all three.",
  model: "All three follow from nitrous oxide being poorly soluble in blood but much more soluble than nitrogen. Little dissolves before the blood is saturated, so the brain level changes fast and onset and recovery are rapid. When the gas stops, it rushes from the blood into the alveoli and dilutes the oxygen there, so we give 100% oxygen to prevent diffusion hypoxia. In a closed air pocket such as a pneumothorax, nitrous oxide enters much faster than nitrogen can leave, so the pocket expands and pressure rises. That is dangerous, which is why the gas is avoided in these patients."
 },
