 questions: [
  ["Benzodiazepines enhance GABA-A receptor function by:","Increasing the frequency of chloride channel opening when GABA binds",["Opening the channel directly in the absence of GABA","Increasing the duration of sodium channel opening","Blocking GABA reuptake into the presynaptic terminal"],"Benzodiazepines bind an allosteric site and make GABA more effective, so the chloride channel opens more often. Direct channel opening at high doses describes barbiturates, a reason they have a narrower safety margin."],
  ["A sedated 40-year-old opens the eyes and follows the instruction 'turn your head' when spoken to, with a light tap on the shoulder. The airway is clear and breathing is adequate without help. The level of sedation is:","Moderate sedation",["Minimal sedation","Deep sedation","General anaesthesia"],"Purposeful response to voice with light touch and an unsupported airway define moderate sedation. A minimally sedated patient responds normally to voice, and a deeply sedated one needs repeated or painful stimulation."],
  ["A sedated 50-year-old responds purposefully only after repeated painful stimulation and needs a jaw thrust to maintain the airway. The most accurate description of the sedation level is:","Deep sedation",["Moderate sedation","Minimal sedation","Anxiolysis"],"Purposeful response only after repeated or painful stimuli, with possible need for airway help, marks deep sedation. A patient in general anaesthesia would not be arousable at all, even with pain."],
  ["A 62-year-old becomes unresponsive to voice with slowed breathing after an IV benzodiazepine. The airway has been supported and oxygen given. The most appropriate drug and initial adult dose is:","Flumazenil 0.2 mg IV over 15 seconds",["Naloxone 0.2 mg IV over 15 seconds","Flumazenil 5 mg IV over 15 seconds","Adrenaline 0.5 mg IM into the thigh"],"Flumazenil competitively reverses benzodiazepines; it starts at 0.2 mg and is titrated to a usual maximum of 1 mg. Naloxone reverses opioids rather than benzodiazepines, so it is the tempting wrong antidote here."],
  ["A patient recovers after flumazenil given for benzodiazepine oversedation. Which of the following best explains why continued monitoring is required?","Resedation can occur as flumazenil wears off before the benzodiazepine is eliminated",["Flumazenil causes delayed anaphylaxis in most patients","Flumazenil converts the benzodiazepine to an active opioid","Flumazenil permanently destroys GABA-A receptors"],"Resedation is reported in a minority of patients, particularly after larger benzodiazepine doses, because the antagonist's effect can end first. Monitoring continues to detect this, not to watch for an expected allergy."],
  ["The rapid onset and recovery of nitrous oxide are best explained by its:","Low solubility in blood",["High solubility in blood","Extensive hepatic metabolism","Strong binding to haemoglobin"],"A poorly soluble gas saturates blood quickly, so alveolar and brain levels change fast. Nitrous oxide is not significantly metabolised and is transported as free gas, not bound to haemoglobin."],
  ["At the end of a nitrous oxide appointment a 45-year-old feels light-headed and has a headache. Which of the following best explains why 100% oxygen is given?","Nitrous oxide leaving the blood dilutes alveolar oxygen",["Oxygen chemically neutralises nitrous oxide in the plasma","Oxygen speeds hepatic metabolism of nitrous oxide","Nitrous oxide binds haemoglobin and must be displaced by oxygen"],"Rapid outward diffusion dilutes oxygen in the alveolus: diffusion hypoxia. AAPD advises at least 5 minutes of 100% oxygen. Nitrous oxide does not bind haemoglobin, which is the distractor's false premise."],
  ["Nitrous oxide should be avoided in a 37-year-old with a pneumothorax because the gas:","Enters the air space faster than nitrogen leaves, increasing its volume and pressure",["Is metabolised to a toxic product by damaged lung","Reacts with pleural fluid to produce nitric acid","Causes bronchospasm in most patients with chest disease"],"Nitrous oxide is about 30 times more soluble than nitrogen, so it enters closed gas spaces faster than nitrogen can leave. The issue is physical expansion, not metabolism or chemical reaction."],
  ["A 29-year-old vegan with untreated vitamin B12 deficiency requests nitrous oxide for restorative care. Which of the following best explains why it should be avoided?","Nitrous oxide inactivates vitamin B12, impairing methionine synthase",["Nitrous oxide binds dietary protein and blocks B12 absorption from the gut","Vitamin B12 deficiency makes the nasal mucosa unable to absorb gas","Vitamin B12 is required for hepatic metabolism of nitrous oxide"],"Nitrous oxide oxidises the cobalt in B12 and inactivates methionine synthase, which can worsen deficiency and neurological or haematological effects. The gas enters through the lungs and is not metabolised by the liver in a meaningful way."],
  ["A 54-year-old takes clarithromycin for a dental infection and later receives triazolam for anxiety. The sedation is deeper and lasts longer than expected. The most likely reason is:","CYP3A4 inhibition reduces triazolam clearance",["Clarithromycin competes with triazolam for GABA-A binding","Triazolam induces clarithromycin metabolism","Clarithromycin reduces triazolam absorption from the gut"],"Clarithromycin inhibits the CYP3A4 that clears triazolam, raising drug level and duration. It does not act on the GABA-A receptor; the interaction is pharmacokinetic."],
  ["Under the RCDSO Standard, a gas delivery system for nitrous oxide sedation must have a fail-safe that does not allow an oxygen concentration below:","30%",["10%","21%","50%"],"The Ontario Standard sets a minimum of 30% oxygen in the delivered mixture. 21% is room air, a tempting distractor, but it is below the standard's threshold."],
  ["A healthy 35-year-old in Ontario receives triazolam 0.25 mg for dental care and feels alert at the end of a 90-minute appointment. Which of the following discharge decisions is most appropriate?","Discharge to the care of a responsible adult after assessing fitness for discharge",["Discharge alone because the patient feels alert","Discharge alone after 5 minutes of 100% oxygen","Discharge alone provided the patient does not drive personally"],"Oral sedation requires discharge to a responsible adult. Only nitrous oxide alone allows dentist discretion for unaccompanied discharge, so feeling alert after triazolam is not sufficient."],
  ["A dentist in Ontario with minimal sedation authorization plans to combine oral triazolam with nitrous oxide at minimal doses. Under the RCDSO Standard, this combination requires:","Oral moderate sedation authorization, regardless of the dose used",["No additional authorization at minimal doses","Only a patient consent form","Only a dental hygienist to monitor the patient"],"Combining an oral sedative with nitrous oxide needs authorization for oral moderate sedation, even if dosing is minimal. Hygienists are not authorized to administer nitrous oxide under this Standard."],
  ["An 82-year-old takes an oral benzodiazepine for dental anxiety at a usual adult dose and is excessively drowsy for many hours. The most appropriate lesson for future appointments is to:","Reduce the dose because older patients are more sensitive to benzodiazepines",["Double the dose so that recovery occurs more rapidly","Add an opioid to shorten the benzodiazepine effect","Give flumazenil routinely before all discharges"],"Older patients often need reduced doses because of altered sensitivity and clearance, as the RCDSO table's caution notes. An opioid would add respiratory depression rather than shorten sedation."]
 ]
